    "body": "MISUMI Americas has launched a foundation with $2 million in seed funding to help students pursue careers in engineering, manufacturing , and supply chain. The MISUMI Imprint Foundation will fund scholarships, hands-on learning programs, and partnerships between schools and manufacturers across the United States.\nWhat’s Related\nThe foundation is MISUMI Group's first corporate giving organization in its 63-year history. Its first grant cycle is expected to open in early 2027, and the company plans to expand the foundation internationally over time.\nMISUMI is also inviting its global suppliers to contribute through a Partner Network. Participating suppliers will make voluntary annual contributions and join an advisory board that meets twice a year to set grant priorities and jointly announce awards.\nThe foundation will direct grants toward three areas:\nScholarships and access: Need-based aid for first-generation and underrepresented students attending vocational schools, trade programs, and universities .\nHands-on learning: Equipment for manufacturing labs, engineering team sponsorships, maker spaces, and student hackathons.\nSchool and industry partnerships: Coursework based on manufacturing problems, internships, apprenticeships, mentoring, and career guidance .\nRELATED STORIES: MISUMI Launches Americas Business to Bring Parts Under One Roof | MISUMI Americas CEO Dave Evans Discusses the Importance of Building Fast With AI\nHelping students start manufacturing careers\nMISUMI, which supplies mechanical components and assemblies, says the foundation responds to hiring difficulties it sees among both customers and manufacturing partners. Unfilled machinist, programmer, and technician jobs can limit how much work a manufacturer can accept, according to the company.\nThe foundation’s focus extends beyond engineers to the people who build, maintain, and inspect equipment. MISUMI cited demand for CNC programmers, toolmakers, quality and maintenance technicians, automation specialists, and production supervisors.\n“MISUMI has spent decades supplying the components engineers design into machines, so we know exactly who builds the physical world,” said Dave Evans, president and CEO of MISUMI Americas.\nEvans said the approach draws on designer Buckminster Fuller’s concept of a trim tab, a small secondary rudder that helps turn a large ship.\n“You don’t change a system by moving all of it at once. You find the small lever in the right place and you keep pushing. A scholarship that lets one student finish a degree is a small thing. Thousands of them, over a decade, are not.”\nBringing suppliers into the grant program\nThe supplier program offers three participation levels: Founding Partner, Growth Partner, and Community Partner. MISUMI did not disclose the contribution amounts for each tier or how much individual grants will provide.\nThe arrangement gives suppliers a way to contribute through an existing foundation without creating their own giving organizations. Schools, other organizations, and suppliers can find information about grants and participation through MISUMI’s Imprint platform.\n“For more than sixty years, MISUMI has supported the people who make things,” said Arata Shimizu, CEO of MISUMI Group. “The Imprint Foundation is the first time we have built an organization dedicated to the next generation of builders.”",
